(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("anacreontea",[{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"The Anacreontea"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<figure class=\"reader-figure\"><img class=\"reader-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bust of Anacreon, Vatican Museum, Rome<\/figcaption><\/figure>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"THE ANACREONTEA"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"[The MS Has the title: &lsquo;Convivial Half-Iambics of Anacreon of Teos and Anacreontea and Trimeters&rsquo;; and at the end of the book &lsquo;End of the Convivial Poems of Anacreon&rsquo;; Anacreontea may be an alternative title, Trimeters that of a section which the scribe failed to copy out.]"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"1"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The Teian lord of minstrelsy<br>Hailed me gaily in a dream;<br>I ran and kissed him tenderly.<br>Old he was, but fair did seem,<br>Fair and fond of merriment;<br>Love his tottering footsteps led,<br>His lip of wine was redolent.<br>There was a garland on his head;<br>He snatcht it off and held it out;<br>It smelt of him; and (silly lout!)<br>I took and bound it my fool brows above;<br>And from that hour I&rsquo;ve never ceased to love."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"[Introductory. Probably the introduction to the book from which the oldest of the poems were selected.]"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"2"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Comrades, give me Homer&rsquo;s lyre,<br>But change the chord of blood and fire;<br>[Another Introduction. Lit. without the murderous string; i.e.. eliminate the warlike element.]<br>&ldquo;Bring cups to-day<br>Of laws, not wine,<br>That so I may<br>The drink divine<br>Mingle in<br>Due &lsquo;rithmetic,<br>Not too thin<br>Nor yet too thick.<br>[Lit. bring me cups of ordinances, and let me mingle rules — perhaps a proem to the book from which some of these songs were taken.]<br>I&rsquo;ld tippling be<br>And dance and sing (But decently)<br>To th&rsquo; merry string.<br>[lit. that I may dance tipsy, and may shout the drinking-song singing to the lyres in a decent frenzy.]<br>Comrades, give me Homer&rsquo;s lyre,<br>But change the chord of blood and fire."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"3"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Come, best of limners, lend an ear<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To th&rsquo; lyric Maid&rsquo;s decree:<br>Limn me the towns the palm that bear<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For laughter and for glee,<br>Limn me the jolly landscape where<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Pied Bacchanals be rovers,<br>And, if the pencil so may dare,<br>The pasture-lands of lovers."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"4"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Take your tools, but make for me,<br>Vulcan, no silver panoply;<br>For what care I for war&rsquo;s array?<br>Make me the deepest cup you may.<br>No stars upon it, if you please,<br>Arcturus nor the Pleiades,<br>Nor yet the Wain; Orion grim,<br>What have I to do with him?<br>But grave me on &lsquo;t the clambering vine<br>And the laughing clusters fine,<br>And, gathering them, a Maenad crew;<br>And make a winepress on it too,<br>And three gold figures treading there,<br>Love, Bacchus, and my fairest fair."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"5"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Graver famous, graver feat,<br>Make a cup the Spring to greet.<br>The season that to man hath sent<br>The rose, that sweetest ornament,<br>Pray duplicate in silver line<br>To make my drinking sweet and fine.<br>By all that&rsquo;s holy I adjure ye<br>Grave me no unfamiliar story;<br>But grave me Bacchus, son of Jove,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To whom the jocund Maenad cries,<br>Grave me the Mystagogue of Love,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Chantress of epithalamies;<br>Then Cupids (but unarmed, I pray)<br>And the Graces laughing gay;<br>Last, &lsquo;neath leafy vine outspread<br>With clusters dangling overhead,<br>Some urchins dancing in a ring<br>Fit toys for Phoebus&rsquo; dallying."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"6"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Weaving a crown of posies<br>I found Love in the roses;<br>By th&rsquo; wings I caught him up<br>And popped him in the cup;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Then took and quaffed<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;It at a draught;<br>And now, my heart within,<br>His wings play tickle-chin."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"7"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;You&rsquo;re old, Anacreon,&rsquo;<br>The ladies say; &lsquo;look on<br>&lsquo;Your forehead in the glass, and see<br>&lsquo;How thin your love-locks be."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"As for my hair, I wot&rdquo;<br>Not whe&rsquo;r &rsquo;tis thin or not;<br>But this I know, the nigher Death&rsquo;s day<br>The more should old men play."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"8"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Give me not Gyges&rsquo; Sardian gold;<br>Kings may keep their wealth untold;<br>But give me nard<br>Upon my beard,<br>And roses round my brow.<br>My care&rsquo;s to-day;<br>To-morrow tell who may."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"So while the days are calm and fine,<br>Come and toast the God of Wine;<br>Let cups be tost,<br>Stakes won and lost,<br>Lest sickness come and say<br>&lsquo;The time is up;<br>Put down the festive cup.&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"9"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"By the Gods I conjure thee,<br>Let me drink and drink again;<br>For frenzied, frenzied would I be,<br>O&rsquo;er the blood of mother slam<br>Alcmaeon and the wild-footed<br>Orestes did their frenzy gain;<br>I, by taking no man&rsquo;s head,<br>But by draughts of liquor red — <br>Thus, thus would I be frenzied."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Hercules of yore ran mad<br>With the quiver and the bow<br>Of his friend th&rsquo; Oechalian lad;<br>Mad ran Ajax once also<br>Brandishing the sword and shield<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Of doughty Hector erst laid low;<br>But cup would I and garland wield<br>Leaving sword and bow afield — <br>Thus, thus would I to frenzy yield."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"10"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"How shall I serve you, chattering Swallow?<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Clip your wayward wings about?<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Or Tereus follow<br>And your glib tongue cut out?<br>Why should your matin talk above<br>Rob my sweet dreams of my love?"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"11"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"This morn I spied a peddling lout<br>That hawked a waxen Love about.<br>I went to him; &lsquo;How much?&rsquo; I said<br>In his own tongue he answered<br>&lsquo;Ony &lsquo;s the price that ye shall pay,<br>&lsquo;Gin I forget him frae this day;<br>&lsquo;For I&rsquo;m nae toyman; all I&rsquo;m willin&rsquo;<br>&lsquo;Is to rid me of a villain.&rsquo;<br>&lsquo;Then take a shilling,&rsquo; answered I,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lsquo;A shilling for a pretty elf.&rsquo;<br>And now, Love, haste and make me fry,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Or you shall melt in flames yourself."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"12"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;Twas Cybele the fair, &rsquo;tis said,<br>That o&rsquo;er the mountains raving made<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Half-woman Attis rove;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"And who by holy Clarus&rsquo; hill<br>Taste laurell&rsquo;d Phoebus&rsquo; wordy rill<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Do shrieking madmen prove."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"And what shall be my madding-stuff?<br>A surfeit sweeter than enough<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Of wine and myrrh and love."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"13"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"I dearly long to love. One day<br>Love bade me do &lsquo;t;<br>I, like a fool, said nay."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"He took his quiver gold-bedight <br>And bow to boot,<br>And challenged me to fight."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Like Peleus&rsquo; son cuirass I sought<br>And armament<br>Of shield and spear, and fought."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"He shot, I ran; his shafts all sped,<br>Angered he sent<br>Himself at me instead."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"He pierced my inmost heart, and laid<br>Me wasted quite;<br>My buckler&rsquo;s useless made."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"For what is outward equipage,<br>When he the fight<br>Doth still within me wage?"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"14"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"If you can count the leaves of all the trees,<br>The sands of all the seas,<br>Then will I have you Lord High<br>Reckoner be <br>Of loves to me.<br>First twenty loves, nay, thirty-five set down<br>From Athens town,<br>And loves in bunches then from Corinth city<br>(Achaean girls are pretty);"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"To Lesbos, Rhodes, Ionia, Caria come<br>And fifty score&rsquo;s the sum.<br>What? does your poor head swim? there&rsquo;s Syria yet,<br>And don&rsquo;t forget<br>Egypt, nor Crete, where all wares may be had<br>And Love runs mad.<br>Shall I add loves beyond Gadire that fall,<br>Ind, Bactria, and all?"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"15"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Whence come you, Dove so dear,<br>Whence through the airy sphere<br>Your course have bent<br>All balm-besprent?<br>What is your errand here?"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;Anacreon&rsquo;s business brings<br>&lsquo;This way my wandering wings<br>&lsquo;To &lsquo;s heart&rsquo;s delight,<br>&lsquo;Bathyllus hight,<br>&lsquo;Now king of all his kings."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;Of Venus bought he me,<br>&lsquo;His courier to be,<br>&lsquo;For one poor song;<br>&lsquo;He says ere long<br>&lsquo;He&rsquo;ll set his courier free."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;E&rsquo;en so, in servitude<br>&lsquo;I&rsquo;ll stay: o&rsquo;er wold and wood<br>&lsquo;Why vagrant it<br>&lsquo;On trees to sit &lsquo;<br>And feed on wild-birds&rsquo; food?"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;Now, bread&rsquo;s my meat in store<br>&lsquo;From his own hands, and more,<br>&lsquo;Red wine I sup<br>&lsquo;From out the cup<br>&lsquo;Where he hath supped before."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;Then to his verse&rsquo;s chime<br>&lsquo;I clap my wings in time;<br>&lsquo;And soon, when sleep<br>&lsquo;His eye doth steep,<br>&lsquo;Dream on his lyre sublime. [over]"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;Now, Sir, I&rsquo;ve said my say;<br>&lsquo;You&rsquo;ve made me prate all day;<br>&lsquo;No talking crow &lsquo;Would chatter so;<br>&lsquo;Enough; pray go your way.&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"16"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Come, master of the Rhodian art<br>And draw the darling of my heart;<br>She&rsquo;s absent, but your paint lay on<br>To her swain&rsquo;s dictation.<br>Make soft and black the hair of her<br>And, if brush may, to smell of myrrh;<br>Make her full-face, the locks of jet<br>Over ivory temples set;<br>Her eyebrows neither join nor sever,<br>But make (as &rsquo;tis) that selvage never<br>Clearly one nor surely two;<br>Her glance be fire (no mimic hue)<br>Like Pallas grey, like Venus tender;<br>For her cheeks and nose to render<br>Mingle rose-leaves with the cream;<br>And that the lip like hers may seem,<br>Make it what Persuasion&rsquo;s is,<br>Provocation to a kiss;<br>And then beneath a shapely chin<br>Let every Grace fly out and in<br>About a marble throat; the rest<br>Be in a chastened purple drest,<br>But let her flesh peep here and there<br>The lines of beauty to declare.<br>You&rsquo;ve limned her to the life, so take your price;<br>You, colours, will be speaking in a trice."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"17"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Limn me thus the lad I love:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Sleek and shining make his hair,<br>Dark beneath, sun-bright above,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;And let the love-locks free as air<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Lie as they will, disordered, there;<br>Make his forehead soft as dew<br>And wreathe &lsquo;t with brows of snaky hue;<br>For&rsquo;s dark eyes mix fierceness bright<br>With a calm and gentle light,<br>This from the mighty War-God brought,<br>That in Cytherea sought,<br>This to affright us when he looks,<br>To keep us that on tenterhooks.<br>Sample for his downy cheek<br>In a rosy apple seek;<br>For the blush that on it lies,<br>Take, as you may take, Modesty&rsquo;s;<br>The lip, I know not how you&rsquo;ll draw &lsquo;t"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"With softness and persuasion fraught,<br>But let the silent colours be<br>A speaking taciturnity.<br>So far the face; let the neck&rsquo;s charms<br>Out-ivory Adon&rsquo;s; breast and arms<br>From Mercury take; let Pollux tell ye<br>Where to get thighs, and Bacchus belly;<br>Where those tender thighs commence<br>Mix love with shamefast innocence.<br>Your art&rsquo;s a niggard; else it would<br>Add back to front, and better good.<br>What need to tell how&rsquo;s feet to make?<br>Enough; the fee&rsquo;s whate&rsquo;er you&rsquo;ll take.<br>Lift yon Phoebus from his nail;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;There Bathyllus&rsquo; pattern is;<br>And if to Samos e&rsquo;er you sail,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Take Phoebus&rsquo; portraiture from his."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"18A"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Give me the Wine-God&rsquo;s bowl,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Ladies; I would drink deep:<br>These fever-fostering hours<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Do make me weep."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Give me the Wine-God&rsquo;s flowers<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;My burning brow to cover;<br>But what can shade the soul<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Of fevered lover?"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"18B"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Beneath Bathyllus&rsquo; shade I&rsquo;ll sit;<br>&rsquo;Tis prettiest of trees,<br>And soft the dainty sprays of it<br>Toss on the breeze;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Beside it sweet Persuasion&rsquo;s brook<br>Goes peacefully;<br>What wayfarer so fair a nook<br>Gould see and pass it by?"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"19"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Young Love the Muses nine<br>Bound once in flowery twine<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;And made him Beauty&rsquo;s slave;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Now Venus would him free,<br>And ransom brings; but he<br>Grown used to slavery<br>His mistress will not leave."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"20"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Anacreon&rsquo;s wine is sweet enough,<br>And Sappho&rsquo;s sweet may be;<br>But add a drop of Pindar&rsquo;s stuff<br>Before you fill for me.<br>These three together mixed, methinks,<br>Would draw Gods from above;<br>Bacchus would quaff this drink of drinks,<br>Bright Venus, yea, and Love."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"21"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Earth drinks the brook, and tree<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The earth; and even so<br>The sea the river, sun the sea,<br>And moon the sun.<br>Then why make ye,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;My comrades, this ado,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;If I &lsquo;ld be drinking too?"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"22"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"A stone on Phrygia&rsquo;s hills, they say,<br>Was daughter of an Argive king;<br>Pandion&rsquo;s child once passed away<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To be a swallow on the wing.<br>My wish it were your glass to be,<br>That you might ever gaze on me;<br>And I would be your lawny vest,<br>That you might aye be in me drest;<br>And I would turn to watery wave<br>That I your pretty cheek might lave;<br>And then I&rsquo;ld fain become, my dear,<br>A box of nard to anoint your hair;<br>Then pearl for throat, then silken twine<br>Swelling bosom to confine;<br>Then sandal, and pray don&rsquo;t forget<br>On your sandal foot to set."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"23"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Of Atreus&rsquo; sons I &lsquo;ld sing,<br>Of Cadmus tell;<br>My lute<br>For all but Love is mute."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"When once I changed each string,<br>Then lyre as well,<br>Fain of Alcides&rsquo; might<br>To indite,"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Love&rsquo;s chime alone would ring.<br>Ye great, farewell!<br>My lyre<br>Love only doth inspire."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"24"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Nature gave horns to bull and hooves to horse,<br>Gave lions ravening jaws, gave hares swift course,<br>Made fish to swim, birds fly,<br>Man to be wise.<br>Then passed she woman by?<br>Nay, gave her, strong as any sword or shield,<br>Beauty, to whose fair eyes<br>Both steel and flame do yield."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"25"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"You come, dear Swallow, with each Spring,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;And build and stay awhile;<br>Each autumn sends you on the wing<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To Memphis or the Nile.<br>But Love, alas! within my breast<br>Hath got an ever-building nest;<br>And one chick&rsquo;s well-nigh fledged, and one<br>Unhatched, another&rsquo;s callow grown,<br>And gaping younglings ne&rsquo;er give o&rsquo;er<br>Their chirping infant cries, and more,<br>The lesser by the great are fed,<br>And all no sooner featherèd<br>Than these with those do mate, and lay<br>New eggs and rear fresh broods.<br>What can I do? I can&rsquo;t away<br>With Love in multitudes."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"26"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Thebes doth your verse employ,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Another&rsquo;s, frays of Troy;<br>My tale shall be<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;The Sack of Me."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"No ships were my undoing,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Nor horse nor foot my ruin,<br>But barbarous foes<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;With eyes for bows."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"27"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"By&rsquo;s mark your horse you&rsquo;ll own,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;By&rsquo;s hat a Parthian&rsquo;s known;<br>When I a lover see,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;He&rsquo;s straightway known to me;<br>For in his soul doth stand<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;A certain little brand."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"28"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"When beside the Lemnian fire<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Venus&rsquo; spouse of iron wrought<br>The arrows of desire,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Venus the sweetest honey sought<br>And dipt the arrows in it; but her boy<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;With bitter gall the honey did alloy."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"When great Mars with massy spear<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;One day returning from the fight<br>Flouted Love&rsquo;s puny gear,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Quoth Love &lsquo;You shall not find it light.&rsquo;<br>Mars took it, but cried out (while Venus smiled)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lsquo;Take &lsquo;t back; &rsquo;tis heavy.&rsquo;<br>&lsquo;Keep it,&rsquo; said the child."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"29"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Woe &rsquo;tis to love not, and to love is woe;<br>But worst it is of woes<br>To love and lose.<br>Goes birth for aught in Love&rsquo;s account? Oh no,<br>Nor disposition; wit,<br>Love tramples it."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Pelf&rsquo;s all they&rsquo;ll see; perish who loved it first!<br>For hence is lost us brother,<br>Father, mother;<br>Hence wars and murders got, and, what is worst,<br>Through love of pelf die we<br>That lovers be."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"30"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"I dreamt I went with wings away<br>And fled<br>The little God;<br>And though his pretty feet were shod<br>With lead He caught his prey.<br>What means this dream? to me full plain It is:<br>By loves so many<br>Harried before nor caught by any,<br>By this<br>I&rsquo;m prisoner ta&rsquo;en."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"31"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"With rushy rod <br>The little God<br>Struck me and bid me follow"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Through rivers quick<br>And copses thick<br>O&rsquo;er hill I sweat and hollow."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"My labouring breath<br>Was nigh to deaths<br>But with his dainty wing then"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"He smites my brow <br>And cries &lsquo;How now?<br>&lsquo;Is love so hard a thing, then?&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"32"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"On lotus-leaves and myrtles fine <br>I&rsquo;ll lean, and the Love-lad<br>In apron clad<br>Shall stand and serve me wine."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Like wheels our running lives are sped,<br>And lie we shall and must<br>A little dust<br>Of bones uncémented."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Why at my grave your unguents pour?<br>Why vain anoilment give?<br>While yet I live<br>Embalm my forehead o&rsquo;er."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Bring roses, and some maiden fair;<br>For ere to join I go <br>The rout below,<br>I fain would banish care."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"33"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;Twas at the mid of night,<br>Whenas the Wain doth wheel<br>Close on Arcturus&rsquo; heel,<br>And every mortal wight"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Is sunk in slumber; then<br>One stood my gate beside<br>And knocked. &lsquo;Who&rsquo;s there?&rsquo; I cried,<br>&lsquo;Who rends my dreams in twain?&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Says Love (&lsquo;t was he) &lsquo;Pray let<br>&lsquo;Me in, nor send his ways<br>&lsquo;A babe forlorn that strays<br>&lsquo;This night so dark and wet.&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Eftsoons I fetched a light,<br>And opening did descry<br>A babe, but winged to fly<br>With bow and arrows dight."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"By th&rsquo; ingle then and there <br>I set him, chafed amain<br>His hands, and wrung the rain<br>From out his dripping hair."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"And when he found him warm,<br>&lsquo;Go to, let&rsquo;s try together&rsquo;<br>Says he &lsquo;if this foul weather<br>&lsquo;Hath done my bowstring harm.&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"This said, he drew the string,<br>And straight with madding arrow<br>Had pierced my very marrow;<br>Then laughing loud took wing,"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"And cried as off he flew<br>&lsquo;Rejoice, my friend, with me;<br>&lsquo;My bow is sound, I see,<br>&lsquo;And pain&rsquo;s in store for you.&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"34"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Sweet Cricket, here&rsquo;s a health to you,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;While on the high tree-top you sing,<br>Made merry with a drop of dew,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As happy as a king."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"For all the landscape hath is yours<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Whate&rsquo;er in farm or field you see;<br>And all the gifts of all the Hours<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;You hold in simple fee."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"You&rsquo;re friends with them that plant and sow<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Because you take nor prize nor prey;<br>You&rsquo;re dear to all men, for we know<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;From you that it is May."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The Muses love you, pretty thing,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;And great Apollo loves you too;<br>For they that make all musicking<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Gave your sweet voice to you."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Time flies, but age can wear you not,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Deft minstrel-offspring of the sod;<br>Sans blood and passions blood-begot<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;You&rsquo;re more than half a God."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"35"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Once on a day, rose-leaves among,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Young Love did fail to see<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A sleeping bee,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;And in the hand was stung.<br>He shrieked, and running both and flying<br>Sped to fair Venus&rsquo; side And &lsquo;Mother&rsquo; cried,<br>&lsquo;Out, out, alas! I&rsquo;m dying.<br>&lsquo;A little snake that goes with wings<br>&lsquo;And as a bee is known &lsquo;To th&rsquo; simple clown,<br>&lsquo;Hath bit me.&rsquo;<br>&lsquo;If such things,&rsquo;<br>His mother answered, &lsquo;make you woe,<br>&lsquo;What then do you suppose<br>&lsquo;Can be the woes<br>&lsquo;Of them you harry so?&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"36"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"If wealth of gold<br>Gave mortals breath,<br>Then I should hold It, that if<br>Death Should come to me,<br>Then I might say &lsquo;Take your fee<br>&lsquo;And go your way.&rsquo;<br>But if his years No mortal buys,<br>Then wherefore tears,<br>And wherefore sighs?<br>If we must die<br>Doth gold avail?<br>Rather may I<br>Drink good brown ale<br>With my best friends,<br>And when day ends,<br>Go to bed Love-shepherded."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"37"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"One night begun with joy of wine,<br>&lsquo;Neath coverlet incarnadine<br>Methought, as nimble light and gay<br>I ran a goal with girls at play,<br>Some boys as Bacchus smooth and soft<br>Pierced my heart with tauntings oft<br>For sporting with fair maidens so.<br>Then I for kisses sued, and lo!<br>They all were fled from out my slumber&rsquo;s ken,<br>And left alone I wept to sleep agen."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"38"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Let&rsquo;s quaff the cheering wine<br>And praise its Lord divine."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Inventor of the measure,<br>True lover of the lyre,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mate of Desire,<br>And Cytherea&rsquo;s pleasure,<br>He gave the Wassail birth<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;And midwived Mirth,"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Killed Pain, and Sorrihed<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Did put to bed;<br>So we, when mixèd bowl<br>The dainty lads do bring<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To th&rsquo; storm-winds fling<br>All sickness of the soul."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Let&rsquo;s drink then, me and you,<br>And give our thoughts relief;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;From pain and grief<br>What profit doth accrue?<br>No mortal man may see<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Futurity; — <br>I&rsquo;ll e&rsquo;en put cup to lip<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;And measures trip,<br>Pour balm and play my fill<br>With pretty girls or boys;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;With all annoys<br>Concern himself who will."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Let&rsquo;s quaff the cheering wine<br>And praise its Lord divine."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"39"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"An old man merry gives me joy,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;I love a dancing boy;<br>If the old man dance boys among,<br>Though&rsquo;s hair be old, his heart is young."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"40"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Since I am mortal made Life&rsquo;s path to tread,<br>What&rsquo;s past I know,<br>But not what&rsquo;s yet to go."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Cares, let me be; with you I&rsquo;ve naught to do.<br>With wine I&rsquo;ll play,<br>Laugh, dance, till end of day."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"41"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"O merry &rsquo;tis to stray<br>Where meads are green and gay,<br>And where the gentle<br>West Blows sweetliest,<br>To see the mantling vine<br>And &lsquo;neath its leaves recline<br>With a fair maid whose breath<br>Love perfumeth."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"42"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"I love old Bacchus&rsquo; antic ring,<br>I like with lads to sweep the string<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;And drink the merry night away;<br>But most of all I&rsquo;m fain to set<br>Flower-de-luce for coronet<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;On maidens&rsquo; brows and share their play;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"No murderous envy knows my heart,<br>I shun the caviller&rsquo;s random dart,<br>I hate the quarrel o&rsquo;er the wine;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"A life of feast and dance and song<br>With maidens fresh and maidens young,<br>A life of gentle joy be mine."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"43"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Roses we&rsquo;ve twined, and roses we<br>About our brows have knit;<br>We laugh as merry as can be<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;And tipple it."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"And now a pretty maiden comes<br>With ivy-tressèd thyrse,<br>And to the lyre she trips<br>Delicately.<br>A lad with lovely ringlets thrums<br>The strings, and doth rehearse<br>With honey-breathing lips<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Gay melody."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Fair Bacchus, Venus fair, and her sweet son,<br>The boy with hair like gold,<br>Do join them blithely to the fun<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Belov&rsquo;d o&rsquo; th&rsquo; old."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"44"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The rose belov&rsquo;d of Loves, the rose<br>Let&rsquo;s mingle with the wine;<br>Let&rsquo;s quaff and laugh and round our brows<br>The sweet-leav&rsquo;d roses twine.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For O!<br>&rsquo;Tis darling of the Spring, the rose,<br>&rsquo;Tis Heaven&rsquo;s dearest thing, the rose;<br>When Venus&rsquo; brat wi&rsquo; th&rsquo; Graces three<br>A-dancing goes,<br>Around his love-locks he<br>Doth bind the rose.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Then ho!<br>Bring garlands, and the lyre shall grace<br>The Wine-God&rsquo;s holy place;<br>With some plump lass I&rsquo;ll fling my toes<br>Crowned with the rose, the rose."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"45"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Whene&rsquo;er the wine I drink<br>My cares to sleep do sink.<br>What then of cares or tears reck I,<br>What reck I then of toils<br>And coils?<br>If willy-nilly I must die,<br>Wherefore<br>Over life&rsquo;s riddle pore?<br>Let&rsquo;s drink fair Bacchus&rsquo; best,<br>For then our cares find rest."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"46"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"See how at break of Spring<br>The Graces rosebuds fling,<br>See how the stilly waves repose,<br>See how the duck a-diving goes,<br>And crane takes wing."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Hot sun drives clouds away;<br>The fields of man look gay;<br>The olive-tree doth push her bud;<br>The fruit that pures the wine-crown&rsquo;d flood<br>Shows leaf and spray."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"47"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"I&rsquo;m old, in sooth,<br>But I can outdrink youth."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"If there be dancing tow&rsquo;rd I&rsquo;ll trip the sward<br>Like old Silene among his pack,<br>And take for sceptre (staff I&rsquo;ll lack)<br>The skin that holds the wine."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"He that doth take delight <br>In fray or fight,<br>Let him go to; he&rsquo;s free to do &lsquo;t;<br>Cup, ho! for me; and pour into &lsquo;t"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The sweetness of the vine.<br>I&rsquo;m old, in sooth,<br>But I can outdrink youth."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"48"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"At Bacchus&rsquo; entering <br>Cares go to bed;<br>I&rsquo;m rich as Sardis&rsquo; king,<br>Rare songs would sing,<br>With ivy crown my head;<br>In thought I put<br>The whole world underfoot.<br>Then drink prepare, my lad,<br>The wine-cup bring,<br>For I far rather had<br>Lie drunk than dead."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"49"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"When Bacchus son of Jove,<br>Who frees the mind of pain,<br>Enters this heart of mine<br>And frenzy brings along,<br>I learn to trip the measure;<br>And there&rsquo;s a sweeter pleasure<br>For lovers of the vine:"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"After the dance and song<br>There come the joys of Love<br>Come let me dance again."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"50"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When I drink wine,<br>A God doth straight begin<br>To warm my soul within;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When I drink wine,<br>Cares, plots, devices go<br>Where the wild sea-winds blow;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When I drink wine,<br>The God that loves the lay<br>Thrills me and makes it May;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When I drink wine,<br>With flowers I garland me<br>And sail life&rsquo;s calmest sea;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When I drink wine,<br>I balm upon me fling,<br>Make love, and Cypris sing;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When I drink wine,<br>The bumpers ope my heart I<br>In routs to bear my part;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When I drink wine,<br>The gain&rsquo;s my own to keep;<br>All share in death&rsquo;s long sleep."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"51"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Nay, shun me not when you discern<br>My locks of gray;<br>Nor, for that you<br>Are in youth&rsquo;s own heyday,<br>My love-gift spurn;<br>But see how true<br>&rsquo;Tis, e&rsquo;en of posies,<br>That lilies white look best &lsquo;mid roses."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"52A"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Why teach me laws and rules,<br>And logic of the schools?<br>What to me, pray,<br>Are all these strings<br>Of words that useless prove?<br>Teach me the gentler things,<br>Wine, and to play<br>With golden Love."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"52B"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The gray hairs on my head<br>Shall serve for crown;<br>Bring wine, boy, mix the bowl,<br>And o&rsquo;er my soul<br>Let stupefaction fall.<br>Awhile empall<br>My living corse; the dead<br>Desire hath none."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"53"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"When I young blood do see,<br>My youth returns to me;<br>Then imp&rsquo;d am I<br>The dance to ply,<br>Then mads me Cybelè."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Bring me the roses red,<br>And let me wreathe my head;<br>I&rsquo;ll slough my years,<br>And peer with peers<br>Foot it comminglèd."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Fetch Bacchus&rsquo; juice, and you<br>Shall see what age can do,<br>How tell his crack,<br>And quaff his sack,<br>And keep good manners too."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"54"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The bull which here you see,<br>The king of Gods must be;<br>A Tyrian maid he hath<br>On &lsquo;s back; a path<br>His hooves do cleave him o&rsquo;er<br>The wide sea&rsquo;s floor;<br>No bull from herd would rove,<br>To pass the sea, but Jove."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"55"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Now that the Spring<br>Has brought the posies,<br>Coy comrade, let me sing<br>A song of roses."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The dainty rose is this,<br>God&rsquo;s darling, mortal&rsquo;s joy,<br>The wreathèd Love-lads&rsquo; bliss,<br>Great Venus&rsquo; toy;<br>&rsquo;Tis every buxom Grace&rsquo;s glory,<br>&rsquo;Tis theme of Poesy and Story."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"On wooing or winning days<br>By briary paths &rsquo;tis sweet<br>To fondle flowers that raise<br>Light fancy&rsquo;s heat;<br>Without the rose what would befall<br>The Wine-God&rsquo;s rite convivial?"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Of Nymphs with arms rose-red,<br>Of Venus&rsquo; roseate cheeks,<br>Of Dawn rose-fingerèd<br>The poet speaks;<br>But others who no poets be<br>Do pleasure find in this same tree."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"For this the sick doth aid,<br>This guards the coffined corse;<br>E&rsquo;en Time by this is made<br>To yield perforce,<br>For roses old in years as well<br>As roses young do sweetly smell.<br>And now shall I<br>To you recall<br>The rose&rsquo;s high Original?"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"When wet from the blue sea<br>Came Venus, when Jove&rsquo;s head<br>Brought forth the War-Lady<br>That was Heav&rsquo;n&rsquo;s dread,<br>Then too first bloomed from out the earth<br>This cunning work, this marvellous birth."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"And that the same, once born,<br>Like Gods might ever live,<br>When Bacchus to the thorn<br>The rose did give,<br>He &lsquo;still&rsquo;d Heav&rsquo;n&rsquo;s nectar o&rsquo;er the tree,<br>And so &lsquo;t has immortality."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"56"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The mighty God that makes<br>The labourer never stale,<br>Young lovers never fail,<br>Feat dancers o&rsquo;er wassail,"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Has come down for our sakes,<br>And brought that philtre fine,<br>That liquor anodyne,<br>The offspring of the vine,"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Keeping it safe as yet<br>Enfettered in the fruit<br>Upon the twining shoot,<br>That when they take knife to &lsquo;t"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Mankind may never get<br>Or ill of body bright<br>Or ill of gentle sprite<br>Till the new vintage-rite."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"57"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"And hath some moulder made the sea?<br>And hath some art in ecstasy<br>Poured the ocean on a dish?<br>And hath some brain half-devilish<br>Sweet Venus on the blue engrav&rsquo;n,<br>Dear Genesis of peopled heav&rsquo;n?<br>Naked (but what&rsquo;s not to see<br>The waves conceal), like tangle free<br>Along that smooth and summer way<br>She brings her soft limbs into play,<br>And leaves a wake of plashing spray.<br>&lsquo;Twixt rosy breast and shapely chin<br>A great wave comes dividing in;<br>She through the furrowed calm goes shining<br>Lily like &lsquo;mid violets twining.<br>O&rsquo;er the silver surface wide,<br>On dolphin-revellers perched astride,<br>A slily-smiling vanguard ride,<br>Passion, Desire and Love;<br>While tumbling the waves above<br>Bow-back&rsquo;d fishes, following<br>The laughing swimmer in a ring,<br>Give her frolic convoying."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"58"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"When truant Gold away doth wing<br>Swift as the wind (&rsquo;tis no rare thing)<br>I go not after him; for who<br>Game he hateth will pursue?<br>I go within, fling care to th&rsquo; breeze,<br>Take lute and troll love-melodies.<br>But when my pride takes heart of grace,<br>Then lo! the truant&rsquo;s in his place,<br>And drugs my wayward wit till I<br>Forget the dulcet quill to ply.<br>Fie, faithless Gold! your cozenings fail;<br>The strings afford me more regale.<br>Love of envy and deceit,<br>That&rsquo;s what you give man for meat;<br>The lute doth mix him happier cheer,<br>Desire of bowers and kisses dear.<br>Play me truant when you will;<br>My lyre shall be my comrade still.<br>Your wiles on those you&rsquo;re free to use<br>Who be no neighbours of the Muse;<br>With sweepers of the string like me<br>The Muse keeps ever company<br>If you would stir a leaky pot, you may,<br>Or take a taper to the light of day."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"59"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Men and maidens shoulder-high<br>Bring the vine&rsquo;s swart progeny,<br>Cast it in the press, and then<br>(Not the maidens but the men)<br>Tread the grape and free the wine,<br>To the Vintage-Lord divine<br>Shouting songs of jubilee<br>When foaming into butt they see<br>The jolly must, which elders taking<br>Trip it with old limbs a-quaking,<br>Trip it with gray locks a-shaking;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"And if youth, when wine&rsquo;s caress<br>Doth his inmost heart possess,<br>Hath reluctant lass waylaid<br>Where she lies &lsquo;neath leafy shade,<br>Her soft limbs sunk in a day-sleep<br>Which Love suborns (lest she should keep<br>Wedlock waiting) to betray her,<br>He without or plea or prayer<br>His unwilling fair embraces;<br>For when cups do flush young faces<br>Bacchus plays with leg o&rsquo;er traces."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"60A"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"The quivering lute I&rsquo;ll play;<br>Contest there&rsquo;s none to-day,<br>But all who practise will<br>Can win the flower of skill.<br>With ivory point I&rsquo;ll chime<br>And cry the Phrygian rhyme,<br>As swan with plumage hoar<br>Upon Cay&rsquo;ster&rsquo;s shore<br>Flutes to the fifing breeze.<br>Help, Muse, an if &lsquo;t you please,<br>(For tripod, bay, and song<br>To Phoebus do belong)<br>And Phoebus&rsquo; love proclaim<br>And ineffectual flame;<br>For chaste it will appear<br>To each and every ear.<br>His flame did him escape<br>And changed her native shape<br>And stood a rustling tree;<br>And Phoebus, even he,<br>Pursued a maidenhead,<br>When Love&rsquo;s imaginèd<br>Fulfilment lo! was seen<br>To be a leaf of green."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"60B"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Why, my heart, O tell me why<br>This ecstatic frenzy high?<br>Wield some weapon an you will,<br>If you fain would hit to kill;<br>But not the bow with which Queen Love<br>Overcame the Gods above.<br>From famed Anacreon take your cue,<br>He&rsquo;s the pattern bard for you:<br>Pledge the fair in wine with me,<br>But be it wine of poesy;<br>We&rsquo;ll seek the nectar of the vine<br>When the sun too hot shall shine."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"FRAGMENTS OF OTHER EDITIONS"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"61"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Clement of alexandria, Miscellanies: While Anacreon says:"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"For I would fain sing of the dainty Love so blooming with flowery garlands; for he is the master of Gods and the subduer of men;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Euripides writes, &lsquo;Love cometh not upon men alone.&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"62"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Hippolytus against all the Heresies: &lsquo;Is not this,&rsquo; he says, &lsquo;the cup (called κονου) in which my lord drinketh, and whereby indeed he divineth? This is the cup found hidden in Benjamin&rsquo;s sack of corn. And moreover the Greeks, he says, mention it in this wild utterance:"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Bring water, bring wine, lad; make me drunken and drowsy. My cup, speaking with an unspeaking lip, tells me what is in store for me.&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"&lsquo;This,&rsquo; he says, &lsquo;would suffice if only men would understand it, Anacreon&rsquo;s mute cup that nevertheless told a secret mystery,&rsquo; He says that Anacreon&rsquo;s cup was unspeaking, the cup which Anacreon says speaks to him with an unspeaking voice, telling him what is to become of him, etc."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"63"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Scholiast on Euripides Hecuba:<br>Why fleest thou my gray hairs?"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"64"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Gregory of Corinth [on the Ionic dialect]: These writers employ the contracted forms of the imperfects, presents, participles, and indeed nearly all parts of the circumflexed verbs, as in the Anacreontea, for instance:"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"For she seems to hear if one choose to speak."},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"65"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"Himerius, orations [on Hermogenes, Proconsul of Greece]: Now had I need of Teian songs, now had I need of the lyre Anacreon knew how to tune against Love when his beloved flouted him. Then should I have said to these what he said: &lsquo;Outrageous and wicked ones, that know not whom to make the mark of your arrows;&rsquo;"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"and maybe I should have used to them Anacreon&rsquo;s threat to Love. It seems that once when he was enamoured of a fair youth, seeing him neglectful of him he tuned his lyre, and threatened that if Love would not immediately wound the lad for him, he would never again play a tune in his honour."},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"e.g. And I said &lsquo;Outrageous and wicked Love, who knowest not whom to make thy mark, I call the Gods to witness that if thou wound not me the fair Bathyllus forthwith, I will never again play tune in thy honour.&rsquo;"}],[]);})();